    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That goblin metaphor is a bit weird lol, but I get the point. So you're saying I'm basically on the hook for whatever costs they run into if I mess up? Is there a way to limit this? Like, can I put a cap on how much I'd actually have to pay out so it doesn't just become an endless sinkhole of money?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The goblin metaphor may be a bit...unconventional, but it gets the point across, doesn't it?\n\nNow, regarding the cap, it's not uncommon for contracts to include language that limits the amount of damages or expenses that can be claimed. This is often referred to as a \"cap on liability\" or a \"limit on damages.\"\n\nIn the indemnification clause, you might see language like \"reasonable\" or \"actual\" damages, which can help limit the amount of liability. For example, the clause might state that the Disclosing Party is only entitled to recover \"reasonable attorneys' fees\" or \"actual damages\" resulting from the breach.\n\nHowever, it's essential to carefully review the contract and look for any language that might limit your liability. You might also want to consider negotiating with the other party to see if you can agree on a cap or a specific limit on damages.\n\nIt's also worth noting that some contracts might include a \"governance clause\" that specifies the jurisdiction and laws that will govern any disputes arising from the contract. This can help ensure that any disputes are resolved in a way that's fair and reasonable, and might even provide some protection for your own interests.\n\nAs a freelancer, it's crucial",
      "source": "target",
